    full_desc: "Built in 1903 by Fairfield Shipbuilding & Engineering Co. of Glasgow, the Duchess of Fife was 329 tons, 210'4\" in length with a beam of 25' and a draught of 8'6\". She had coal fired boilers and 4 cylinder, triple expansion engines. She was capable of 17.5 knots and in later years could carry up to 1101 passengers.\n
    The Duchess of Fife was the first Clyde paddle steamer to be built by Fairfield, and for the first few years sailed on the seasonal railway connections. \n
    During the First World War she was taken for service as a minesweeper and during the Second World War for convoy duties.\n
    After the wars she took up service to the Holy Loch, and runs to Wemyss By, Largs and Millport.\n
